  "mainEntity": {
    "name": "Why Is Your Garage Floor Coating Peeling After Winter?",
    "text": "Why Is Your Garage Floor Coating Peeling After Winter?",
    "@type": "Question",
    "answerCount": 1,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "text": "Why does a garage floor coating begin to peel, flake, or lift off the slab after a single winter?\n\nIn most cases, peeling comes down to improper slab preparation, trapped moisture, or winter temperature swings. If oil, road grime, or old sealers were not completely stripped before coating, the new product cannot achieve a strong mechanical bond. \n\nAnother frequent culprit is applying coatings outside the required 40°F to 90°F temperature window, or putting down resin over fresh concrete that has not cured for the full 30 days. Moisture vapor pushing up through the slab can also break the bond, causing blisters that peel away once hot tires or winter road chemicals put stress on the film.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\nIf you see wide areas of delamination or flaking concrete under the paint, patching over the damage will not hold. A professional contractor can assess moisture levels, grind away failed coatings down to clean aggregate, and apply a properly formulated system built to resist vehicle traffic and chemicals. \n\nContact Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC by calling 303-505-0866 or emailing Hhremodelingllc1986.ab@gmail.com to restore your garage floor with dependable preparation and finish work.",
      "@type": "Answer",
      "author": {
        "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
        "@type": "Organization"
      }
    }
  }
}